<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
	The application's privacy manifest, held to the built application by
	apps/companion/scripts/privacy-manifest.mjs. The release product is what ships, so the
	categories below are the ones its executable imports, whichever package they come from. The
	Swift packages linked statically (Firebase Messaging and what it needs) also ship manifests of
	their own, and so does the WebRTC framework. docs/releases/app-store-privacy.md ties each entry
	to the code and to the answers the App Store asks for.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
		Tracking. The application does not track. It links neither AdSupport nor
		AppTrackingTransparency, carries no advertising or analytics library (Firebase Messaging is
		the only Google library in it), and sends nothing to a data broker.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<!--
		Data collected. Each entry is data that leaves the device to a service the application
		reaches, in a form that service (or its provider) can read or keep beyond answering the
		request. None is used for tracking, and each is tied to the account or installation that
		sent it. Content the application seals on the device before sending (settings, drafts, the
		recovery bundle, sealed mailbox items) is listed as Other User Content because the services
		keep the sealed bytes, although they cannot read them.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<!-- Signing in to a managed account: the address the person gives the sign-in page. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeEmailAddress</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!-- The account identifier a sign-in returns, and the identifier of the installation. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
			The keys that identify this device to the services (the endpoint key a relay and the
			discovery service see, the installation key that signs requests), and the notification
			registration token Firebase makes for the Apple push token.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeDeviceID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!-- A managed voice call: the person's speech goes from the device to the voice provider. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeAudioData</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
			What the person's own content becomes in a managed service: the text a voice call is given
			and the transcript it returns, and the sealed settings, drafts, recovery bundle and
			mailbox items that settings sync, recovery and the authority feed keep.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUserContent</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!-- The relay bytes, voice seconds and stored bytes the services count against an allowance. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUsageData</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<!--
			File timestamps. The executable imports stat, lstat and fstat, NSFileModificationDate, and
			(from GoogleUtilities' network session, which has its own manifest) NSURLCreationDateKey.

			C617.1, "Declare this reason to access the timestamps, size, or other metadata of files
			inside the app container, app group container, or the app’s CloudKit container.": the
			standard library's file calls, which the pairing records, the attempt budget, the
			descriptor directory, drafts, settings, the recovery record and the sync membership make
			on files and directories in the application's own container (whether a path exists, is a
			file or a directory, its size and its owner and mode), which Tauri makes on the
			application's own resource directory, and which the standard library's backtrace
			symbolizer makes on the application's own executable.

			3B52.1, "Declare this reason to access the timestamps, size, or other metadata of files or
			directories that the user specifically granted access to, such as using a document picker
			view controller.": the dialog plugin's file picker reads the modification date of the file
			the person chose, for the picker's result.

			Two calls read a file that is neither in the container nor one the person chose, and
			Apple's list has no reason for them. Each reads a size or a type and no timestamp.
			libsodium's random source checks with fstat that /dev/urandom is a character device
			(sodium_init, randombytes_stir, randombytes_sysrandom_stir). The Rust standard library
			reads the system version property list at the first availability check (std::fs::read,
			reached from __isPlatformVersionAtLeast).
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
				<string>3B52.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
			System boot time. The voice call adapter reads ProcessInfo.systemUptime to measure how
			long the call's owner has been silent, so that a call whose owner has gone is ended.
			35F9.1, "Declare this reason to access the system boot time in order to measure the amount
			of time that has elapsed between events that occurred within the app or to perform
			calculations to enable timers." Nothing read here, or derived from it, leaves the device.
			The WebRTC framework has its own manifest for its own use.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>35F9.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
			User defaults. The executable imports NSUserDefaults for the Firebase and GoogleUtilities
			packages linked into it, which keep their installation and heartbeat records there; the
			application's own code does not use user defaults. CA92.1, "Declare this reason to access
			user defaults to read and write information that is only accessible to the app itself."
			Those packages declare their own reasons in their own manifests as well.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
